This error happens every time the search client throws an error. I don't agree with @Haroenv and think this should be handled differently.

I updated a local fork to also listen for the "error" event. After this change, everything works fine.

```
const waitForResults = () =>
    new Promise<void>((resolve, reject) => {
      const helper = search.mainHelper!

      let searchReceived = false;
      let recommendReceived = false;

      search.mainHelper!.derivedHelpers[0].once('result', () => {
        searchReceived = true;
        if (!search._hasRecommendWidget || recommendReceived) {
          resolve();
        }
      });
      search.mainHelper!.derivedHelpers[0].once('recommend:result', () => {
        recommendReceived = true;
        if (!search._hasSearchWidget || searchReceived) {
          resolve();
        }
      });

+      // However, we listen to errors that can happen on any derived helper because
+      // any error is critical.
+      helper.on('error', (error) => {
+        reject(error);
+      });
+      search.on('error', (error) => {
+        reject(error);
+      });
+      helper.derivedHelpers.forEach((derivedHelper) =>
+        derivedHelper.on('error', (error) => {
+          reject(error);
+        })
+     );
    });
```

Thank you for taking the time! Unfortunately, the infinite page load still occurs if a catch is not added to the waitForResults.

```
waitForResults(search).then((requestParamsList) => {
        let shouldRefetch = false;
        walkIndex(search.mainIndex, (index) => {
          shouldRefetch =
            shouldRefetch ||
            index
              .getWidgets()
              .some((widget) => widget.$$type === 'ais.dynamicWidgets');
        });

        if (shouldRefetch) {
          waitForResultsRef.current = wrapPromiseWithState(
            waitForResults(search).then(injectInitialResults)
          );
          return;
        }

        injectInitialResults(requestParamsList);
      })
+     .catch((error) => {
+        console.error(error);
+      })
```

When I console the error, I can tell that the problem is probably related to the helper itself. This type error comes from AlgoliaSearchHelper.prototype._dispatchAlgoliaResponse where the content parameter doesn't exist when the search client fails.

<img width="915" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/1bcadb2f-082d-45f7-b1f4-36f5c5b60e59" />

The easiest way to test this is to create a custom search client like this:

```
import { SearchClient } from 'instantsearch.js';

export const searchClient: SearchClient = {
   search(requests) {
      return fetch("http://localhost:3000/api/search", {
         method: "post",
         headers: {
            "Content-Type": "application/json",
         },
         body: JSON.stringify({ requests }),
      })
         .then((res) => res.json())
         .catch((err) => {
            console.error(err);
         });
   },
};
```

In /api/search I throw an error to simulate a failed search. If catch is not added to waitForResults you will get the infinite page loading. If you handle the error (by adding a catch to waitForResults) and log it, you will see the same maximum call stack problem.

Hi there!

After a bit more investigation, I found a trick that fixes the issue for my specific case, but I guess will be breaking widgets in SSR:

```tsx
// instantsearch/packages/react-instantsearch-nextjs/src/InstantSearchNext.tsx
...
  return (
    <ServerOrHydrationProvider
      isServer={isServer}
      instance={instance}
      ignoreMultipleHooksWarning={ignoreMultipleHooksWarning}
    >
      <InstantSearch {...instantSearchProps} routing={routing!}>
        {/* Commenting this line stops the loop (obviously, as it is the cause...) */}
        {/* isServer && <InitializePromise nonce={nonce} /> */}
        {children}
        {isServer && <TriggerSearch nonce={nonce} />}
      </InstantSearch>
    </ServerOrHydrationProvider>
  );
}
```

I realised I am not adding much to the conversation, but in the end, it is clear that the issue is isolated in that `InitializePromise` component.

So far I was not able to really understand 100% what it does, and therefore I was not able to debug and try to fix it. Claude was not able either, so I commented the line and now my app _kind of_ works.

I'll share more info whenever I can.
